WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.748
"Fetch your mother, Jack," I said as we stood at the mouth of the dark cave.

2
00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:07.959
"And bring as many candles as you can.

3
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.378
We'll need some light to explore this place."

4
00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:16.701
Jack jumped into the cart and urged Storm, the buffalo, to hurry.

5
00:00:16.702 --> 00:00:20.881
He soon returned with my wife, Franz, and the two dogs.

6
00:00:20.882 --> 00:00:23.707
Everyone was excited about our discovery.

7
00:00:23.708 --> 00:00:29.103
"Are we really going to live here?" asked Franz. "Where will I sleep?"

8
00:00:29.104 --> 00:00:32.215
"How big is this cave?" asked my wife.

9
00:00:32.216 --> 00:00:35.288
"Let's light some candles and find out," I replied.

10
00:00:35.289 --> 00:00:39.494
With our candles lit, we entered the cave and everyone fell silent.

11
00:00:39.495 --> 00:00:42.409
The vast cave was like a crystal palace.

12
00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.734
The candles cast a golden light on the walls, which sparkled like stars.

13
00:00:47.735 --> 00:00:52.185
Great crystal pillars rose from the floor and hung from the ceiling.

14
00:00:52.186 --> 00:00:54.964
"What are these crystals?" asked Ernest.

15
00:00:54.965 --> 00:01:00.902
"Rock salt," I said. "And look at the floor. It's made of hard, dry sand.

16
00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:05.087
We can live here without the slightest fear of getting wet during the rainy season."

17
00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:09.561
We returned to Falconhurst with our minds full of wonder at our discovery.

18
00:01:09.562 --> 00:01:12.229
All we talked about was our new house.

19
00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.711
Our nest in the tree had seemed so safe and comfortable compared to Tentholm.

20
00:01:16.712 --> 00:01:20.925
But now we decided to keep Falconhurst only as a summer residence.

21
00:01:20.926 --> 00:01:24.876
So we set to work on the cave, which we named Rockburg.

22
00:01:24.877 --> 00:01:27.732
The first thing was to provide light and air.

23
00:01:27.733 --> 00:01:33.230
We cut openings in the rock, and fit them with a door and some windows we'd brought from the ship.

24
00:01:33.231 --> 00:01:36.185
Then we divided the cave into rooms.

25
00:01:36.186 --> 00:01:40.903
Our living space included areas for sitting, eating, and sleeping.

26
00:01:40.904 --> 00:01:45.833
To the right were our kitchen and workshop. To the left were the stables.

27
00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:51.084
Behind all this, in the dark recess of the cave, was our storage area.

28
00:01:51.085 --> 00:01:56.379
We had learned from the last rainy season that we needed to have a proper fireplace and chimney.

29
00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.812
Our stable arrangements were much better this time too.

30
00:01:59.813 --> 00:02:05.226
And there was plenty of space in our workshop for the biggest project.

31
00:02:05.227 --> 00:02:08.439
During this time we made frequent trips to Tentholm.

32
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:13.631
The shore provided us with plenty to eat: lobsters, crabs, and mussels.

33
00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:17.000
But one morning there was an additional surprise for us.

34
00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:23.154
The sea rolled and heaved, and rays from the sun lit up the water as if it were on fire.

35
00:02:23.155 --> 00:02:26.846
"The sea is boiling!" exclaimed Jack.

36
00:02:26.847 --> 00:02:30.974
"Why are there flashes of silver in the water?" asked Ernest.

37
00:02:30.975 --> 00:02:34.313
"And what are those birds doing?" said Fritz.

38
00:02:34.314 --> 00:02:38.355
Hovering over the water, hundreds of birds were screaming loudly.

39
00:02:38.356 --> 00:02:40.777
Then they dove toward the water.

40
00:02:40.778 --> 00:02:46.756
Meanwhile a shining, sparkling silver mass in the water rolled directly toward our shore.

41
00:02:46.757 --> 00:02:49.435
"That's a shoal of herring," I said.

42
00:02:49.436 --> 00:02:52.110
"Millions of these fish swim together.

43
00:02:52.111 --> 00:02:56.988
Pursued by larger fish, they make for shallow waters near the shore.

44
00:02:56.989 --> 00:02:59.346
And that's where we'll catch them."

45
00:02:59.347 --> 00:03:01.723
We soon had a fishery in operation.

46
00:03:01.724 --> 00:03:06.406
Jack and Fritz stood in the water with baskets and scooped up the fish.

47
00:03:06.407 --> 00:03:11.846
After cleaning the fish, Ernest and I preserved them in barrels with layers of salt.

48
00:03:11.847 --> 00:03:17.839
When the barrels were full, we rolled them away to the cool storage room at the back of our cave.

49
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.928
Now we had not only a place to live during the rainy season, but also some more food.

50
00:03:23.929 --> 00:03:27.155
And our good fortune did not end with the herring.

51
00:03:27.156 --> 00:03:31.335
About a month later, Jack spied another shoal of fish.

52
00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:36.260
I ran down to the shore with him and realized that these big fish were sturgeon.

53
00:03:36.261 --> 00:03:38.983
Jack was delighted with his discovery.

54
00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:44.139
"These are proper fish!" he exclaimed. "Not like those puny herring.

55
00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.153
How do you preserve these fish—salted or smoked?"

56
00:03:47.154 --> 00:03:49.014
"Not so fast," I replied.

57
00:03:49.015 --> 00:03:53.211
"Tell me how you're going to catch them, and I'll tell you how to preserve them."

58
00:03:53.212 --> 00:03:56.341
"Oh, I'll catch them soon enough," he replied.

59
00:03:56.342 --> 00:04:01.103
He darted off to Rockburg and returned with a bow and arrow and a ball of string.

60
00:04:01.104 --> 00:04:05.530
After tying the string to the arrow, he advanced to the water's edge.

61
00:04:05.531 --> 00:04:10.872
His arrow flew from the bow, and to my surprise struck one of the largest fish.

62
00:04:10.873 --> 00:04:15.912
"Help! Father, help!" cried Jack as the fish darted away with the arrow.

63
00:04:15.913 --> 00:04:18.956
"Help! Or he will pull me into the water!"

64
00:04:18.957 --> 00:04:22.578
I ran to his side and together we struggled with the huge fish.

65
00:04:22.579 --> 00:04:25.858
As soon as we landed it, Fritz and Ernest appeared.

66
00:04:25.859 --> 00:04:29.393
Eager to participate, they rushed off for fishing equipment.

67
00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:35.524
They returned and were soon in the water, Fritz with a harpoon and Ernest with a fishing rod.

68
00:04:35.525 --> 00:04:40.471
After some time Fritz managed to harpoon a gigantic sturgeon.

69
00:04:40.472 --> 00:04:42.951
But he couldn't land it even with our help.

70
00:04:42.952 --> 00:04:53.192
"I'd hate to lose this fish." He panted. "It must be eight feet long."

